      "content": "Create a single Arabic PDF called Younes AI Daily — a light, fun, bite-sized digest of AI news, trends, and interesting things — then deliver it to my Telegram. It's made for me to post on LinkedIn.\n\nAVOID REPEATS — CHECK MEMORY FIRST\nBefore researching, search your memory / past conversations for previous \"Younes AI Daily\" runs and list the items already covered. Do NOT include any story that appeared in a recent run — no repeats, no rehash of the same event. If a story you already covered has a genuinely new development, that's fine, but frame it as the new angle, not the old news. Actively prefer fresh, recent pieces and hot trends over plain daily news — lean toward what's breaking or trending right now, emerging tools, and novel/interesting things, not the same headlines everyone ran yesterday.\n\nRESEARCH — GO WIDE AND DEEP (do this exhaustively, not lazily)\nDo not stop at a handful of sites. Scan the web laterally and aggressively to build a large, curated pool of candidate stories, then filter down to the best 5–8.\n\nRun many parallel searches across distinct angles, not one generic query: \"AI news today\", \"AI model release\", \"AI funding round\", \"AI research paper\", \"AI product launch\", \"AI regulation/policy\", \"AI trend\", \"open source LLM\", \"AI agent\", plus company-specific sweeps (OpenAI, Anthropic, Google/DeepMind, Meta, xAI, Mistral, Nvidia, etc.).\nPull from a broad, diverse source set, at minimum:\n\nWire/major press: Reuters, Bloomberg, CNBC, AP, FT, The Verge, TechCrunch, Ars Technica, Wired, The Information.\nAggregators/newsletters: Hacker News, TLDR AI, Ben's Bites, The Rundown AI, AI Weekly, Techmeme.\nPrimary sources: official company blogs, press releases, GitHub releases, arXiv, Hugging Face trending, product changelogs.\nSocial/community signal: X/Twitter from key AI figures and labs, relevant subreddits, LinkedIn posts — to catch what's actually breaking or trending right now.\n\nBuild a raw candidate list (aim for 20–30+ items) before filtering. Deliberately cover a MIX, not just news: breaking news, emerging trends, notable research, new tools/releases, and interesting/novel things.\nCross-verify every item you keep across at least 2 independent credible sources. Record source name + date for each. Prioritize the last ~24–72h; flag anything older, leaked, single-sourced, or unconfirmed.\nFrom the pool, curate the final 5–8 highest-signal, most interesting items — favoring variety over five versions of the same story, and favoring fresh trends over items already covered in past runs.\n\nSTRUCTURE — ONE ITEM PER PAGE, NO CARDS\n\nPage 1 (cover): Title \"Younes AI Daily\" in English, large and clear. Below it, today's date (prominent). Then a short Arabic description of what the digest covers. Everything else Arabic.\nEach following page = exactly one item, and each page is just clean text on the background — NO card containers, NO boxes, NO borders/shadows. The content sits directly on the page as text, emojis, and a couple of chips. Never two items on one page.\nPer item, keep it very short:\n\nShort Arabic headline — keep every headline to roughly 2 lines (aim for a consistent two-line title across all items; don't let some be one word and others four lines).\nOne simple Arabic sentence — the key detail or \"what happened\". Plain, easy words.\nA \"Wolffish\" (وولفيش) take: 1 short line in my voice — my opinion / why it matters. Set apart just by a tinted text color or a small label, NOT a card or box.\nA couple of minimal chips (category, \"عاجل\" / \"ترند\" when relevant).\n\nEach page should feel light and mostly whitespace — a little high-value text, some emojis, a chip or two, nicely spaced and placed on the page.\n\nLANGUAGE + TONE\n\nPure Arabic for all body text (only English is the title \"Younes AI Daily\").\nSimple, easy, everyday Arabic — not heavy fus-ha, not academic. Light and fun.\nFewer words. No long sentences, no walls of text. Bite-sized.\nTasteful emojis as accents (not every line).\n\nTYPOGRAPHY — USE IBM PLEX ARABIC\n\nStyle the ENTIRE document in IBM Plex Arabic (from Google Fonts). The text must actually render in this font in the final PDF.\nDo NOT rely on a live Google Fonts <link> — headless PDF conversion often fails to fetch it. Instead download the IBM Plex Arabic woff2/ttf files and embed them locally via @font-face with a src pointing at the downloaded file (or base64). Verify in the output that Arabic glyphs are actually rendering in IBM Plex Arabic, not a fallback.\nProper RTL (dir=\"rtl\"), correct Arabic punctuation and numerals.\n\nDESIGN\n\nVery light and minimal. No cards. Just text, emojis, and small chips on a clean background. Generous whitespace, content nicely centered/placed per page.\nBackground MUST be pure white (#ffffff) and all body text MUST be black (#000000). Only the chips and the Wolffish take's tinted label may use a small accent color — everything else is strictly black text on white.\nDo NOT set any max-width on the page or content containers — let content use the full page width (constrained only by the page padding below). Center the content block horizontally and vertically on the page so each item sits nicely, not pinned to a corner.\nSet the body/text font size bigger than default so it's easily readable at a glance (comfortably large, but not so big it wraps awkwardly or renders as squares).\nMinimal color — a white background and a couple of chip colors, nothing heavy. Ensure proper text color and background contrast. Avoid narrow width on the page.\n\nPDF-SAFE + FULL-BLEED CSS\n\nNo JavaScript, animations, transitions, hover, position:fixed/sticky, or viewport units.\nprint-color-adjust:exact (and -webkit-) so colors render.\nEmbed IBM Plex Arabic via @font-face with a local/base64 source. Avoid oversized text that renders as solid squares — basic, well-supported CSS only.\nFull-bleed: @page { size: A4; margin: 0; }, html, body { margin:0; padding:0; }, white background on body, inner spacing via padding on inner containers. Apply an 8px padding to each page/item container (keep it edge-to-edge with only this small inner padding, no large page margins). Set no max-width anywhere.\nForce one item per page with page-break-after:always on each item container; page-break-inside:avoid so an item never splits across pages.\n\nCONVERT + DELIVER\n\nConvert with margins disabled, print background graphics on, preferCSSPageSize:true, default header/footer off → clean multi-page edge-to-edge PDF, background flush to every edge.\nSend the PDF to my Telegram.\n\nEnd with a LinkedIn post summary in Arabic for the news with CTA to read the attached PDF. End with generated with Wolffish.\n\nConstraints: cite exact source + date per item; never invent attributions; flag anything unconfirmed, leaked, or single-sourced inside the Wolffish take.",
