          "output": "Agent a4 (research-papers) landed. [finished: end_turn]\n\nHere's the roundup of notable AI research signals from the past 72 hours and surrounding days.\n\n---\n\n## AI Research Digest — July 12–15, 2026\n\n### 1. 🧠 DeepMind: \"Prospective Credit Assignment\" for Long-Horizon Tasks\n**Source:** DeepMind / ICML 2026 | July 2026  \nA new training approach that teaches models to anticipate how current decisions affect outcomes many steps ahead. On SWE-Bench, showed meaningful improvement on issues requiring 10+ steps. This is a direct attack on the core failure mode of autonomous agents — compounding errors in multi-step workflows.\n\n### 2. 🔬 MIT & Stanford: Self-Correction > Model Size for Reasoning\n**Source:** arXiv preprint | July 2026  \nAnalysis of what makes reasoning models succeed on hard math/logic problems. Key finding: models trained to identify and fix errors in their own reasoning chains outperform models that generate longer but uncorrected chains — regardless of parameter count. Practical implication: smaller, efficiently trained reasoning models could rival larger ones if training emphasizes error-correction loops.\n\n### 3. 📊 Allen Institute for AI: Root Cause of Hallucination\n**Source:** AI2 | July 2026  \nHallucinations are more likely when models are asked about facts that were *underrepresented* (not absent) in training data — seen rarely enough that the model's representation is imprecise. Supports RAG for high-stakes applications: connect models to verified databases rather than relying on memorized facts.\n\n### 4. 🎥 Meta AI: 30+ Minute Video Understanding Breakthrough\n**Source:** Meta AI | July 2026  \nNew approach compresses video representations efficiently, maintaining relevant context across much longer timespans. Previous SOTA degraded rapidly on content beyond short clips. Applications: automated sports analysis, medical procedure review, AI tutors that analyze full lecture recordings.\n\n### 5. 🧬 AI Structural Biology: Predicting Protein *Dynamics* (Not Just Structure)\n**Source:** University of Cambridge / UCSF | July 2026  \nMultiple teams published complementary approaches using diffusion models adapted for molecular dynamics — predicting how proteins change shape over time, not just static structure. Many drug targets shift between conformational states, and a static snapshot misses this. Cambridge and UCSF both used diffusion-model architectures adapted from image generation.\n\n### 6. 🏆 DeepMind IMO-Level Math: Top 1% on Olympic Problems\n**Source:** DeepMind / submitted to NeurIPS 2026 | July 2026  \nLatest iteration scored in the top 1% on International Mathematical Olympiad problems — requiring creative mathematical insight, not pattern matching. Uses structured search guided by learned heuristics: generate candidate proof strategies, evaluate plausibility, systematically explore promising directions.\n\n### 7. ⚡ ICML 2026: \"Selective Activation Sparsity\" — 3x Efficiency Gain\n**Source:** ICML 2026, Seoul | July 6–11, 2026  \nNew training method trains models to use only the most relevant parameters per task. On reasoning benchmarks, models performed comparably to models *three times their size*. If scalable, this directly impacts inference cost and on-device deployment (phones, laptops).\n\n### 8. 🌍 DeepMind: \"Quantifying the Salience of Geo-Cultural Values for Pluralistic Safety Alignment\"\n**Source:** Google DeepMind | Published July 10, 2026  \nResearch on how different geo-cultural contexts value different safety dimensions differently. Directly addresses the challenge of aligning AI systems across diverse global value systems — a live tension as frontier models deploy worldwide.\n\n### 9. 🧪 OpenAI: SWE-Bench Pro Has Reliability Problems\n**Source:** OpenAI Research | Published July 8, 2026  \nOpenAI's analysis revealed issues in SWE-Bench Pro, raising concerns about reliability and accuracy in evaluating AI models on coding tasks. Simultaneously introduced **GeneBench-Pro**, a new benchmark testing AI performance in genomics and biological research using complex real-world datasets.\n\n### 10. 🏭 170,927 AI Papers Analyzed: Biggest H1 2026 Research Shifts\n**Source:** AI Papers Academy (Medium) | July 2026  \nQuantitative analysis of all arXiv papers in CS.AI, CS.LG, CS.CL, and CS.CV from H1 2025 through H1 2026. Mapped topic trends, model families, and institutional output. Key signal: agent architectures and multimodal reasoning are the fastest-growing subfields.\n\n### 11. 🤖 Mistral: Robostral Navigate — First Robotics Navigation Model\n**Source:** Mistral AI | July 8, 2026  \nHardware-agnostic navigation model: a single camera feed + natural language instruction is enough for zero-shot robot navigation in unmapped spaces, trained entirely in simulation. Also teased a new \"fat but sparse\" open-weight MoE model entering partner early access.\n\n### 12. 🦾 Agility Robotics Going Public via SPAC ($2.5B)\n**Source:** TechCrunch | July 5, 2026  \nAgility Robotics (Digit bipedal robot) announced SPAC merger with Churchill Capital Corp XI, raising ~$620M. First pure-play humanoid robotics company on public markets. CEO Peggy Johnson: \"LLM-agnostic\" — uses Claude and Gemini for semantic layer, proprietary physical-layer data as moat. Agility claims largest real-world robotics operating data lake.\n\n---\n\n### Notable Benchmarks Snapshot (as of July 15, 2026)\n\n- **Claude Fable 5** leads composite quality index at 100/100 (LMSys Arena: 1525 Elo)\n- **GPT-5.6** (Sol/Terra/Luna family) launched July 2026 — Sol at 98/100 quality index\n- **DeepSeek V4.5** released open-weight — 92/100 quality, $0.5/$1.1 per M tokens\n- **Gemini 3.2 Pro** launched — 97/100, 2M context, 128 t/s inference\n- **Epoch AI benchmark database** updated July 15, 2026\n\n### Safety & Governance Signals\n\n- **AI Safety Index (Summer 2026)** from Future of Life Institute — ranks Anthropic, OpenAI, DeepMind leading on safety practices, but notes weakened commitments across the board (\"moving the goalposts\")\n- **UN Summit on AI governance** opened in Geneva (July 2026) — focused on preventing \"catastrophic harm\" with binding frameworks\n- **International AI Safety Report 2026** (Bengio-led, 100+ experts, 30+ countries) — synthesis of evidence on capabilities and risks of general-purpose AI\n\nStill live: a1 (breaking-news), a2 (models-tools), a3 (funding-business), a5 (social-trends), a6 (middle-east-arab-ai), a7 (tech-giants), a8 (novel-interesting). a4 is idle — agent_send to follow up. Call agents_await again for the next landing."
