          "output": "Agent a8 (novel-interesting) landed. [finished: end_turn]\n\nHere's my compiled research — the juicy stuff from July 2026:\n\n---\n\n## 🐟 Wolffish Research Report — Novel AI Stories, July 2026\n\n### Tier 1: \"Whoa\" — genuinely surprising\n\n**1. The US Government Banned Claude Fable 5 — Then Unbanned It**\n- **Summary:** On June 12, 2026, the US Department of Commerce slapped export controls on Anthropic's Claude Fable 5 and Mythos 5 — an unprecedented government ban on a frontier model. The ban was lifted June 30, and Fable 5 returned to global access July 1 with new classifiers, 50% weekly usage caps, and a \"no access for foreign nationals\" compliance regime. First time the US gov has ever emergency-frozen an AI model.\n- **Source:** Al Jazeera / ChatForest / explainx.ai — July 1, 2026\n\n**2. Alan Turing's Biggest AI Assumption May Have Been Wrong**\n- **Summary:** Computer scientist Peter J. Denning's new book *Turing's Mistake* argues that the entire AI field has been built on two flawed assumptions from Turing's 1950 paper — that intelligence can exist without a body, and that a machine can demonstrate intelligence by imitating conversation. Denning says tacit knowledge (common sense, intuition, culture, practical skills) can never be encoded in software, and LLMs \"only manipulate words, they cannot know or understand the meaning.\"\n- **Source:** ScienceDaily — July 13, 2026\n\n**3. China's AI Companion Law Takes Effect TODAY — Doubao and Qwen Shut Down, Millions Lose Chat Data**\n- **Summary:** China's first national framework for emotionally interactive AI took effect July 15, 2026. ByteDance's Doubao (the country's most popular chatbot with 345M users) and Alibaba's Qwen were forced to shut down custom AI persona features. Bloomberg reports users heartbroken over losing virtual companions they'd bonded with. Data goes read-only through October, then deleted.\n- **Source:** Bloomberg / TechNode / TechTimes — July 6-15, 2026\n\n**4. Centaur: The AI That Predicts Human Behavior Better Than Humans Can**\n- **Summary:** Helmholtz Munich researchers built Centaur by fine-tuning Llama 3.1 70B (modifying just 0.15% of weights) on 10M+ human decisions from 160 psychological experiments. It predicts not just what people will decide but *how long they'll take to decide it*. Its internal representations naturally align with human brain activity patterns — despite never being trained for that. Published in *Nature*. Could simulate experiments that are ethically impossible on real humans.\n- **Source:** Nature / Neural Buddies / BBC Science Focus — April-July 2026\n\n**5. AI Helped Decode Bird Language — Scientist Wins $100K Prize**\n- **Summary:** Dr. Julie Elie at UC Berkeley spent 15+ years decoding zebra finch calls using AI, mapping 11 core calls in their vocabulary. She proved birds categorize sounds by *meaning* not just acoustics — a fundamental discovery about animal cognition. Won the 2026 Coller-Dolittle Prize for two-way interspecies communication. Human-bird conversation may be closer than we think.\n- **Source:** The Guardian / CNN — June-July 2026\n\n---\n\n### Tier 2: \"Huh, that's interesting\" — genuinely cool/weird\n\n**6. Meta Launched and Killed an Instagram AI Feature in 5 Days**\n- **Summary:** Meta rolled out \"Muse\" — an AI feature that let users edit/regenerate photos taken from *public Instagram accounts*. Hollywood talent agencies erupted. Meta pulled it July 10 after days of blistering backlash. The tech worked; the opt-out policy did not.\n- **Source:** TechCrunch / Variety / BBC — July 9-13, 2026\n\n**7. OpenAI Allegedly Hid Billions of ChatGPT Logs from NYT Copyright Suit**\n- **Summary:** The New York Times and Daily News say OpenAI lied about its ability to search ChatGPT training data for copyrighted content. Publishers asked a judge to sanction OpenAI, claiming it hid 20M+ logs and pretended it lacked the technical ability to search them. Federal judge ordered the logs turned over anyway. AI's biggest copyright fight just got dirtier.\n- **Source:** Ars Technica / TechCrunch / Bloomberg Law — July 9, 2026\n\n**8. Tesla Robotaxi Launches in Miami — With Zero Safety Monitors**\n- **Summary:** Tesla launched fully driverless robotaxi service in Miami on July 3 — skipping the human-safety-monitor phase it used in every other city. First time unsupervised rides in a brand-new market from day one. Also the first real test of camera-only FSD in Florida rain. Critics note Tesla's crash rate is still 9x human baseline in Austin.\n- **Source:** TNW / HNGN / Electrek — July 3-6, 2026\n\n**9. The $17,600 Humanoid Robot That Can Dance and Pout**\n- **Summary:** Chinese company UBTECH launched the UWORLD U1 — a lifelike humanoid with 88 degrees of freedom, emotion-aware LLM (20+ emotional states at 90%+ accuracy), and speech-to-lip-sync under 20ms. It looks like a creepy pop star action figure, can dance, do \"pouty catwalk model face,\" and has already taken 13,000 pre-orders at $17,600 each. The Register asked if it can run DOOM.\n- **Source:** The Register — July 2, 2026\n\n**10. The New Yorker Goes Deep on the Humanoid Robot Reality Check**\n- **Summary:** A fantastic long-read about 1X's Neo robot — 5'6\", 66 lbs, beige bodysuit, no face, $20K. 10,000+ people have put deposits on one. But the article's real meat: \"The world still doesn't have a ChatGPT equivalent for a robot.\" Nvidia's robotics lead and Google DeepMind's head of robotics both say home humanoids are years away. Børnich (1X CEO) admits Neo shouldn't be around small children and calls it \"robotics slop.\"\n- **Source:** The New Yorker — July 6, 2026 Issue\n\n**11. AI Could Sharpen Our View of Dark Energy**\n- **Summary:** An AI-powered framework for analyzing Type Ia supernovae doubled the precision of dark energy measurements. Researchers at Imperial College used AI + weak gravitational lensing to produce the sharpest-ever map of the dark universe, with results in \"excellent agreement\" with Big Bang afterglow measurements. AI is becoming a core cosmology instrument.\n- **Source:** Imperial College London / Space.com / ScienceDaily — June-July 2026\n\n---\n\n### Tier 3: \"Oh that's wild\" — offbeat, specific, fun\n\n**12. Anthropic's Claude Tag — An AI That Lives in Your Slack**\n- **Summary:** Anthropic launched Claude Tag on June 23 — an always-on Claude that lives inside Slack as a \"coworker.\" You tag @Claude in any channel and it watches, remembers context, and handles tasks asynchronously across channels. Each channel gets its own isolated Claude identity. The Register called it \"nosy, always-on agentic AI.\" Available for Enterprise/Team customers.\n- **Source:** Anthropic blog / TechCrunch / The Register — June 23, 2026\n\n**13. Hawaii's Attempt to Ban AI Political Satire Got Wrecked in Court**\n- **Summary:** The Babylon Bee sued Hawaii over Act 191 — a law requiring \"materially deceptive\" AI-generated political content to carry warning labels. A federal judge struck it down as unconstitutional. The ruling affirms that satire and parody remain protected speech even when AI-generated. A bellwether for the flood of AI election laws headed to courts.\n- **Source:** Courthouse News / KHON2 — February-July 2026\n\n**14. GPT-5.6 Launches as a Three-Model Lineup (Sol, Terra, Luna)**\n- **Summary:** OpenAI rolled out GPT-5.6 on July 6 as three distinct models: Sol (high-end reasoning/coding, $5/1M input, $30/1M output), Terra (balanced), and Luna (lightweight). First time OpenAI productized tiered reasoning in one release. Meanwhile Grok 4.5 dropped the same morning, trained jointly with Cursor. For the first time since Fable 5's ban, every major frontier lab had a publicly available model simultaneously.\n- **Source:** BuildFastWithAI / Open Data Science — July 6-12, 2026\n\n**15. The Weirdest AI at CES 2026 — A Panda Companion for Grandma**\n- **Summary:** Mind With Heart Robotics showed off An'An — an AI-powered panda companion designed for elderly people with cognitive decline. Not a humanoid — just a fuzzy panda that talks and interacts. Won the \"weird but sweet\" award. Also: Switchbot's Onero H1 robot won \"best robot of CES\" for its weirdly long body and endearingly blank stare.\n- **Source:** Mashable / Engadget — January/July 2026\n\n---\n\n### Honorable mentions that didn't make the cut:\n- **30-46% of enterprise AI token usage at US companies flowing to Chinese models** (CNBC, July 8) — wild stat, but more of a data point than a story\n- **Morgan Stanley warning an AI breakthrough is coming** — too vague\n- **AI-powered emotion detection projects** — standard tutorial fodder\n- **Most \"cool AI project\" lists** — recycled project tutorials, not news\n\n**My top 3 picks for \"would make someone say whoa\":** Centaur (the mind-predicting AI), the Fable 5 government ban, and the China AI companion heartbreak. The Turing Mistake book is the most thought-provoking for people who think deeply about AI.\n\nNo other agents are live. a8 is idle — agent_send to follow up. Call agents_await again for the next landing."
